Title: EcoSmart AI
Subtitle: Helping Nigerian households turn waste into value, and connecting them with verified recyclers.

OVERVIEW
Problem: In Nigeria, recyclable waste is dumped or burned because people don't know what it's worth or who can take it, while recyclers struggle to find reliable supply.
My role: Founder & Product Lead: research, product design, design system and the company website, working with a team of five.
What I built: An AI-powered recycling marketplace that identifies waste from a photo, estimates its potential value, and connects users with nearby verified recyclers.
Outcome: MVP tested by 20+ users, 130+ on the waitlist, and ₦1M in non-dilutive funding from two pitch wins and a grant.
Team: Muhammad Najeeb Yusuf (CTO), Chizaram Obi (Frontend Developer), Sa'adatu Ahmad Shehu (Business Development Lead), Najaatu Abdulkarim (Social Media Manager)
Tools: Figma (design system, final UI), Figma Make (early prototypes), Claude and ChatGPT (writing support, research analysis)
Stats (count up): 57 recyclers researched · 157 households surveyed · 20+ MVP testers · 130+ waitlist

SECTION: "Trust, not willingness, was the real barrier"
We spoke with 57 recyclers and 157 households. Three insights:
1. People don't know their waste has value. Households weren't refusing to recycle. They just didn't know what was recyclable, what it was worth, or where to take it.
2. Recyclers can't find reliable supply. One recycler told us only about 30% of his material came directly from the people who generate it. The rest came through informal middlemen.
3. Trust is the real barrier. One recycler had lost millions of naira to a fraudulent deal, and households worried about fair prices. Any solution had to make every step visible and traceable.

SECTION: "Every step of a waste deal is visible and fair"
Four feature cards:
- AI scan result: the material, recyclable or not, a value range, an "AI verified" or "Self-reported" badge, and a "This doesn't look right?" link
- Nearby verified recyclers with ratings, distance, materials and price ranges
- Pickup requests show only the user's area, not their address, until a recycler accepts
- Fair Final Amount: the recycler enters the weighed amount at their published rate, and the user can accept it or flag it as unfair
Design system block: colour swatches (#599432, #1A6739, #3F7A22, #E6F4D7, #F8CB72) and fonts (Manrope for headings, Inter for body).
[IMAGE PLACEHOLDERS: V1 screens labelled "Tested" – scan, AI result, recyclers list, pickup, Fair Final Amount, recycler dashboard]

SECTION: "The AI earned trust by admitting when it was wrong"
We shared the MVP with 20+ testers from our community and asked them to try it with their own waste.
What worked: Testers found the app easy to use. Account creation and scanning were smooth, and the AI identified materials such as PET bottles, aluminium and cans correctly, letting users confirm or change the category.
Quote (large): "I uploaded an incorrect image first, and the platform correctly flagged it." — MVP tester
What testers struggled with (2-column table: What testers said | What it told us):
- "Search Nearby Recycler" found no recyclers | The marketplace is only as good as its supply; recycler onboarding matters as much as the app
- They wanted to search by area, such as Kubwa | People think in neighbourhoods, not map pins
- The estimated value didn't show how it was calculated | Users trust a number more when they can see the sum (₦/kg × kg)
- PET instructions contradicted each other | Small inconsistencies weaken trust in the whole result
- Some items were missing, like old phones | The category list needs to grow with real user waste
A trade-off we made: Testers asked for automatic location. We kept location off by default, because live location could put users at risk if misused. Convenience mattered, but safety came first.

SECTION: "Keeping every conversation on the platform"
In Version 1, users contacted recyclers by phone call or WhatsApp. Once a conversation left the platform, we couldn't see what was agreed, so we couldn't protect either side if something went wrong.
Decision: We chose in-app calls and chat over WhatsApp and direct calls, because keeping every conversation on the platform lets us trace activity, resolve disputes and protect both users and recyclers.
