SECTION: "Five principles decided every screen"
Five cards, numbered:
1. Private by default. Nothing leaves Kioni unless you copy, print or download it, and every screen says who can see what.
2. Your words, kept as you wrote them. Anything you write about yourself sits on cream "paper". Kioni never rewrites it, and corrections keep a history.
3. Calm, not gamified. No scores, no streak shaming, no red badges. Every result counts, including "Not yet".
4. Small steps, real work. Thirty seconds on a Friday is enough. One question at a time, and anything extra is marked optional.
5. Honest by design. Limits, prices and consequences are shown before you act, never after.

SECTION: "Your words belong on paper"
The visual language separates the person from the tool. White cards hold the things you operate; cream paper holds what you wrote about yourself. Gold only appears for wins and things that need a look. Green means you can act, and red is used for one thing only: delete.
