Title: Ajo Book
Subtitle: A phone record book for ajo holders: who has paid, whose turn it is, and reminders, without members downloading anything.

Intro line (above the overview, for readers outside Nigeria): Ajo is a savings group: members pay a fixed amount every day, week or month, and take turns collecting the whole pot. One trusted person, the holder, keeps the records.

OVERVIEW
Problem: Ajo holders, mostly women aged 30 to 60 who aren't very digital, keep records in a notebook and post a WhatsApp list of names with ✅ to prove who has paid. Flipping pages, chasing late payers and remembering whose turn it is causes stress and arguments.
My role: Solo designer and builder: interviews, UX and UI, the "Market Ledger" design system, directing the build in Bolt with AI, and testing with real holders.
What I built: A mobile web app that works offline. With one tap the holder marks who has paid, shares the ✅ list on WhatsApp, reminds people by WhatsApp, SMS or a call, and tracks turns, swaps and payouts. Members download nothing, and no money moves through the app.
Outcome: Version 1 built in one weekend and live. Submitted to Bolt's Builders Week (October 2026). Now used by a holder who runs many groups, and her feedback is shaping Version 2.
Timeline: Version 1: 17–18 October 2026. Version 2: in progress since November 2026.
Tools: Bolt.new (React, TypeScript, database), Claude (research, design feedback, prompts and testing), Figma, Phosphor Icons, Google Fonts, CapCut
Stats (count up): 7 holders interviewed · 2 days to build Version 1 · 3 ways to remind (WhatsApp, SMS, call) · 0 downloads needed for members

SECTION: "The competitor wasn't another app. It was her notebook."
I interviewed 7 women in my community who hold ajo, one on one, and watched how they actually record payments. Every one used a notebook and a WhatsApp ✅ list.
Findings:
1. The notebook sets the bar. Marking a payment had to be faster than writing a name and a tick.
2. The ✅ list is a trust ritual. Holders post it so everyone can see it's fair. The app should make that list, not replace it.
3. Not every member has a smartphone. Reminders had to work by SMS and calls, not only WhatsApp.
4. Holders aren't very digital. Small text, icons without words and hidden gestures would lose them.
5. The network is unreliable and data costs money. It had to work offline.
6. Money is personal. Holders wanted help with the records, not an app that touches their money.
[QUOTE PLACEHOLDER: one short quote from an interview, no real name]
[IMAGE PLACEHOLDERS: a notebook page and a WhatsApp ✅ list, with names blurred]

SECTION: "Only the holder needs the app"
Most ajo apps make every member sign up or move money through the app, which leaves out people with basic phones. Ajo Book is built for the holder alone.
Comparison table (columns: App | Who uses it | Moves money? | Members must sign up?):
- Notebook + WhatsApp | Holder | No | No
- Ajo (ajo-app.com) | Holder and members | No | Yes, by invite link
- My Ajo App | Members | Yes (2% fee) | Yes
- Akawo Manager | Professional collectors | No | No (₦7,000–60,000 a month)
- Ajo Book | Holder only | No | No (free for up to 3 groups)

SECTION: "Designed for a busy market day, not a desk"
The "Market Ledger" design system looks like a paper record book with Nigerian adire indigo, not like a bank app.
Five principle cards, numbered:
1. Easier than the notebook. Marking a payment is one tap.
2. Words with every icon. No icon stands alone.
3. Nothing hidden. No swipes or long-presses; every action is a visible button.
4. Forgive, don't warn. Undo after an action instead of "Are you sure?" popups.
5. Works on a bad day. Offline, poor network, bright sunlight.
